.TH LSIIO 1 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lsiio \- list industrial I/O devices and triggers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \%lsiio
.RB [ \-v ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \%lsiio
lists the devices and triggers of the kernel's industrial I/O (IIO)
subsystem, as found in
.IR /sys/bus/iio/devices .
.PP
For each directory there named
.BI iio:device N
it prints
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
Device \fINNN\fP: \fIname\fP
.EE
.in
.PP
and then, for each directory named
\fBtrigger\fP\fIN\fP,
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
Trigger \fINNN\fP: \fIname\fP
.EE
.in
.PP
where
.I NNN
is the device or trigger number
.IR N ,
zero-padded to three digits, and
.I name
is the first word of the entry's
.I name
attribute.
All devices are listed before all triggers.
Entries are listed in the order the directory is read, not sorted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
Also list the input channels of each device:
the names of the files in the device directory that begin with
.B in_
and end with
.B _raw
or
.BR _input ,
one per line and indented, followed by an empty line.
The option may be given more than once, with no further effect.
.PP
Any other option, or any non-option argument, is an error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line; a usage message is printed to standard error.
.PP
Otherwise, if
.I /sys/bus/iio/devices
cannot be opened, or the
.I name
attribute of a device or trigger cannot be read,
.B \%lsiio
exits with a nonzero status.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/bus/iio/devices/
Directory scanned for IIO devices and triggers.
.TP
.IR /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:device N /name
Name printed for each device.
.TP
.IR /sys/bus/iio/devices/trigger N /name
Name printed for each trigger.
.SH NOTES
.I /sys/bus/iio/devices
exists only when the kernel's IIO subsystem
.RB \%( CONFIG_IIO )
is built in or its module is loaded, and sysfs is mounted on
.IR /sys .
If the directory cannot be opened,
.B \%lsiio
prints
\(dqNo industrial I/O devices available\(dq
to standard error.
.PP
An IIO device registered without a name has no
.I name
attribute.
When
.B \%lsiio
cannot read a
.I name
attribute it stops at that entry, without printing a diagnostic,
and exits with a nonzero status.
.SH SEE ALSO
The IIO sysfs interface and the kernel's IIO userspace tools are described
in the Linux kernel source in
.PP
.I \%Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs\-bus\-iio
.br
.I \%Documentation/iio/iio_tools.rst
